{
  "summary": "Adds the `p2p_1p1c_liveness` fuzz target to exercise opportunistic one-parent-one-child (1p1c) package relay end-to-end. The target initializes a full PeerManager, validation engine, and active chainstate against fuzzer-controlled peers to assert that an honest peer announcing an acceptable package child eventually gets it accepted into the mempool regardless of adversarial peers stalling, disconnecting, malleating transactions, or delivering witness-stripped variants.",
  "problem": "Existing transaction download manager fuzz targets mock and model validation results rather than running real validation logic. Consequently, they cannot verify how actual validation return states (such as reconsiderable fee failures, missing inputs, and witness malleations) interact with cross-peer download accounting and reject filters to prevent transaction censorship.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: 'The existing download-manager targets model validation results, so they cannot check how real results are attributed across peers.'",
    "Commit 3b16af7e7a notes that without targeted testing and invariants, bugs where a malleated parent from one peer cancels orphan resolution for another peer can easily survive tens of thousands of fuzz iterations ('reverting the witnessless-parent fix survived 57k runs')."
  ],
